public class BinaryTreeLevelOrderTraversal {

    public static void levelOrderTraversal(TreeNode root) {
        if (root == null) {
            return;
        }

        Queue<TreeNode> queue = new LinkedList<>();
        queue.offer(root);

        while (!queue.isEmpty()) {
            TreeNode node = queue.poll();
            System.out.print(node.val + " ");

            if (node.left != null) {
                queue.offer(node.left);
            }

            if (node.right != null) {
                queue.offer(node.right);
            }

            // Skip duplicate nodes at the same level
            while (!queue.isEmpty() && queue.peek().val == node.val) {
                queue.poll();
            }
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Example usage
        TreeNode root = new TreeNode(1);
        root.left = new TreeNode(2);
        root.right = new TreeNode(2);
        root.left.left = new TreeNode(3);
        root.left.right = new TreeNode(4);
        root.right.right = new TreeNode(3);

        levelOrderTraversal(root);
    }
}
```

In this implementation, we use a queue to store the nodes of the tree and perform a level order traversal. The queue initially contains the root node. We process each node by printing its value and then adding its left and right children to the queue. 

To handle duplicate nodes, we check if the value of the next node in the queue is the same as the current node's value. If it is, we skip that node by removing it from the queue. This ensures that we only print duplicate nodes once during the level order traversal. 

The time complexity of this solution is O(n), where n is the number of nodes in the binary tree. The space complexity is O(1) as we are not using any additional data structures to store the nodes."}]

