import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class PrimeNumbersInRange {
    public static List<Integer> findPrimesInRange(int start, int end) {
        boolean[] isPrime = new boolean[end + 1]; // Initialize a boolean array to track prime numbers

        // Initially, assume all numbers are prime
        for (int i = 2; i <= end; i++) {
            isPrime[i] = true;
        }

        // Apply the Sieve of Eratosthenes algorithm
        for (int i = 2; i * i <= end; i++) {
            if (isPrime[i]) {
                for (int j = i * i; j <= end; j += i) {
                    isPrime[j] = false; // Mark multiples of i as non-prime
                }
            }
        }

        List<Integer> primeNumbers = new ArrayList<>();
        // Print all the prime numbers within the given range
        for (int i = Math.max(start, 2); i <= end; i++) {
            if (isPrime[i]) {
                primeNumbers.add(i);
            }
        }

        return primeNumbers;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int start = 10;
        int end = 100;

        List<Integer> primeNumbers = findPrimesInRange(start, end);

        System.out.println("Prime numbers between " + start + " and " + end + ":");
        for (int prime : primeNumbers) {
            System.out.print(prime + " ");
        }
    }
}
```

This algorithm uses the Sieve of Eratosthenes, which works by iteratively marking the multiples of each prime number as non-prime. It initializes a boolean array `isPrime[]` to track the prime numbers. Initially, all numbers are assumed to be prime. The algorithm starts from 2 and iterates up to the square root of the given range. For each prime number `i`, it marks all its multiples as non-prime. Finally, it collects and prints all the prime numbers within the given range.

The time complexity of this algorithm is O(n*log(log(n))) where n is the given range. The space complexity is O(n) since the boolean array `isPrime[]` is used to track the prime numbers."}]

