```java
import java.util.PriorityQueue;

public class KthLargestElement {
    public static int findKthLargest(int[] nums, int k) {
        PriorityQueue<Integer> pq = new PriorityQueue<>();

        for (int num : nums) {
            pq.offer(num);

            if (pq.size() > k) {
                pq.poll();
            }
        }

        return pq.peek();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] nums = {4, 8, 3, 10, 6};
        int k = 2;

        int kthLargest = findKthLargest(nums, k);

        System.out.println(kthLargest);
    }
}
```

Explanation:
- We use a `PriorityQueue` to store the k largest elements from the array.
- We iterate over the array and add each element to the `PriorityQueue`.
- If the size of the `PriorityQueue` exceeds k, we remove the smallest element (since we want the kth largest).
- Finally, we return the smallest element remaining in the `PriorityQueue`, which is the kth largest element.

The time complexity of this solution is O(n log k) because adding an element to the `PriorityQueue` takes O(log k) time and we do this for each element in the array. Additionally, removing the smallest element from the `PriorityQueue` also takes O(log k) time, but we only do this k times."}]

