```java
public class SelectionSort {

    public static void sort(int[] arr) {
        int n = arr.length;

        for (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {
            int minIndex = i;
            for (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {
                if (arr[j] < arr[minIndex]) {
                    minIndex = j;
                }
            }
            swap(arr, i, minIndex);
        }
    }

    private static void swap(int[] arr, int i, int j) {
        int temp = arr[i];
        arr[i] = arr[j];
        arr[j] = temp;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] arr = {5, 4, -2, 10, 2, 5, 4, -2};
        sort(arr);
        System.out.println(Arrays.toString(arr));
    }
}
```

Explanation:

The `sort` method takes an array `arr` as input and performs the selection sort algorithm on it. It iterates through the array and finds the minimum element in the unsorted portion of the array, and then swaps it with the element at the current index. This process is repeated until the entire array is sorted.

The `swap` method is a helper method that swaps the elements at indices `i` and `j` in the array.

In the `main` method, we create an example array `arr` and call the `sort` method to sort it. Finally, we print the sorted array using `Arrays.toString(arr)`.

The output of the above code will be:

```
[-2, -2, 4, 4, 5, 5, 10]
```

This implementation handles duplicate elements by swapping them to the leftmost position in each iteration of the selection sort algorithm. Therefore, duplicate elements will appear next to each other in the sorted array."}]

