```java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(digits(1234));
    }

    public static int factorial(int n) {
        if (n == 0) {
            return 1;
        } else {
            return n * factorial(n - 1);
        }
    }

    public static double digits(int n) {
        String strN = Integer.toString(n);
        char[] digits = strN.toCharArray();

        ArrayList<Integer> oddDigits = new ArrayList<>();
        ArrayList<Integer> evenDigits = new ArrayList<>();

        for (char c : digits) {
            int digit = Character.getNumericValue(c);
            if (digit % 2 == 0) {
                evenDigits.add(digit);
            } else {
                oddDigits.add(digit);
            }
        }

        if (evenDigits.size() == 0 && oddDigits.size() != 0) { // all digits are odd
            int half = oddDigits.size() / 2;
            int sum = 0;
            for (int i = 0; i < half; i++) {
                sum += oddDigits.get(i);
            }
            return factorial(sum);

        } else if (oddDigits.size() == 0 && evenDigits.size() != 0) { // all digits are even
            int half = evenDigits.size() / 2;
            int product = 1;
            for (int i = 0; i < half; i++) {
                product *= evenDigits.get(i);
            }
            return Math.sqrt(product);

        } else {
            int sumOfEvenSquare = 0;
            for (int i : evenDigits) {
                sumOfEvenSquare += Math.pow(i, 2);
            }

            int sumOfOddFactorial = 0;
            for (int i : oddDigits) {
                sumOfOddFactorial += factorial(i);
            }

            return sumOfEvenSquare + sumOfOddFactorial;
        }
    }
}
```

Note that there are a few differences due to language constraints:

- In Java, there is no built-in function to calculate the product of a list, so we need to use a loop.

- In Python, you can use a negative index to get an element from the end of the list, but it's not possible in Java. So we need to explicitly calculate the index to get the middle two elements.

- Python has the feature of creating a list comprehension, which is not there in Java. So, in Java equivalent code, we need to use traditional for loop for the same."}]

