```java
public class QuickSort {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] array = {10, 7, 8, 9, 1, 5};
        int n = array.length;
        quickSort(array, 0, n - 1);
        System.out.println("Sorted array: ");
        printArray(array, n);
    }

    // Fix: swap the check condition to low < high instead of low > high
    static void quickSort(int[] array, int low, int high) {
        if (low < high) {
            // Fix: first find pivot and then call recursively quicksort on two halves
            int pi = partition(array, low, high);

            quickSort(array, low, pi - 1);
            quickSort(array, pi + 1, high);
        }
    }

    static int partition(int[] array, int low, int high) {
        int pivot = array[high]; 
        int i = low - 1;
        // Fix: If array[j] <= pivot then increase "i" and swap array[i] and array[j] 
        for (int j = low; j < high; j++) {
            if (array[j] <= pivot) {
                i++;
                swap(array, i, j);
            }
        }
        // Swap pivot with the element at i + 1 to get it in the correct place
        swap(array, i + 1, high);

        return i + 1;
    }

    // Use a helper method to swap two elements in an array
    static void swap(int[] array, int i, int j) {
        int temp = array[i];
        array[i] = array[j];
        array[j] = temp;
    }

    static void printArray(int[] array, int size) {
        for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {
            System.out.print(array[i] + " ");
        }
    }
}
```

The time complexity of Quicksort is O(n^2) in the worst-case scenario when the array is already sorted, but it can be improved to O(n log n) on average if a good pivot is chosen. The worst-case scenario for Quicksort happens rarely in randomized pivot choice. 

