7. Radix sort
8. Counting sort
9. Bucket sort

Each of these algorithms have their own strengths and weaknesses and can perform better in certain situations based on the data input.

Here is the implementation of a Merge Sort algorithm in Java:

```java
public class MergeSort {
    void merge(int arr[], int left, int mid, int right) {
        int n1 = mid - left + 1;
        int n2 = right - mid;

        int leftArray[] = new int[n1];
        int rightArray[] = new int[n2];

        for (int i=0; i<n1; ++i) {
            leftArray[i] = arr[left + i];
        }

        for (int j=0; j<n2; ++j) {
            rightArray[j] = arr[mid + 1+ j];
        }

        int i = 0, j = 0;

        int k = left;
        while (i < n1 && j < n2) {
            if (leftArray[i] <= rightArray[j]) {
                arr[k] = leftArray[i];
                i++;
            } else {
                arr[k] = rightArray[j];
                j++;
            }
            k++;
        }

        while (i < n1) {
            arr[k] = leftArray[i];
            i++;
            k++;
        }

        while (j < n2) {
            arr[k] = rightArray[j];
            j++;
            k++;
        }
    }

    void sort(int arr[], int left, int right) {
        if (left < right) {
            int mid = (left + right)/2;

            sort(arr, left, mid);
            sort(arr , mid+1, right);

            merge(arr, left, mid, right);
        }
    }
}
```

The best-case time complexity for this merge sort method is O(n log(n)) and worst-case is also O(n log(n)). It is important to note that it may not always be possible to implement a custom algorithm that performs better than these traditional algorithms for all types of data inputs. The efficiency of an algorithm can often depend on the nature of the data being sorted. 

Also, conventional sorting algorithms have been studied extensively over the years and are highly optimized in terms of time complexity."}]

