[{"role": "assistant", "content": "Here, we would be implementing the quicksort algorithm which has an average time complexity of O(n log n), and it’s one of the fastest sorting algorithms for average-case inputs:

```java
public class Main {

    static int partition(int[] array, int low, int high) {
        int pivot = array[high];  
        int i = (low-1);
        for (int j=low; j<high; j++) {
            if (array[j] <= pivot) {
                i++;

                // swap array[i] and array[j]
                int temp = array[i];
                array[i] = array[j];
                array[j] = temp;
            }
        }

        int temp = array[i+1];
        array[i+1] = array[high];
        array[high] = temp;

        return i+1;
    }

    static void quickSort(int[] array, int low, int high) {
        if (low < high) {
            int pi = partition(array, low, high);
            quickSort(array, low, pi-1);
            quickSort(array, pi+1, high);
        }
    }

    public static void main(String args[]) {
        int[] array = {4, 9, 3, 6, 2, 1};
        int n = array.length;
        quickSort(array, 0, n-1);
        for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i)
            System.out.print(array[i] + " ");

        System.out.println();
    }
}
```

This program sorts the array using the quicksort method, which involves selecting a pivot and partitioning the other elements into two sub-arrays, according to whether they are less than or greater than the pivot. The sub-arrays are then recursively sorted. 

Therefore when we run this code, we get the output: 1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 9 which is the sorted version of our original array."}]

