```java
public class Main {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String[] arr = {"bar", "baz", "foo", "quux"};
        String target = "foo";

        int result = binarySearch(arr, target);

        if (result == -1) 
            System.out.println("Target not present in array");
        else 
            System.out.println("Target found at index " + result);
    }

    public static int binarySearch(String[] arr, String target) {
        int l = 0, r = arr.length - 1;
        while (l <= r) {
            int mid = l + (r - l) / 2;

            int res = target.compareTo(arr[mid]);

            // Check if target is present at the mid index
            if (res == 0) 
                return mid;

            // If target greater, ignore left half
            if (res > 0) 
                l = mid + 1;

            // If target is smaller, ignore right half
            else 
                r = mid - 1;
        }

        return -1;
    }
}
```

Please note that for binary search to work, the array should be sorted. If the array is not sorted, then we should sort it first before performing the binary search. In the given example, the array is sorted in ascending order.

Binary search works by dividing the array into two halves about the middle element and then checking if the target element is less than, greater than or equal to the middle element. If the target is equal to the middle element, then the index of the middle element is returned. If the target is less than the middle element, then the right half of the array is ignored and the same process is applied to the left half. If the target is greater than the middle element, then the left half of the array is ignored and the same process is applied to the right half. This process repeats until the target element is found or until the entire array has been searched."}]

