[{"role": "assistant", "content": "Your function needs to take into account the varying number of days in each month, as well as the occurrence of leap years (where February has 29 days instead of 28). The GregorianCalendar class in Java can handle these adjustments for us. Here's how you could write the function:

```java
import java.util.Calendar;
import java.util.GregorianCalendar;
import java.text.SimpleDateFormat;
import java.text.ParseException;

class Solution {
    public String validDatePlus(String date, int days) {
        SimpleDateFormat format = new SimpleDateFormat("MM-dd-yyyy");
        format.setLenient(false); 
        try {
            Calendar calendar = new GregorianCalendar();
            calendar.setTime(format.parse(date));
            calendar.add(Calendar.DAY_OF_MONTH, days);
            return format.format(calendar.getTime());
        } catch (ParseException e) {
            return null;
        }
    }
}
```

This code first creates a SimpleDateFormat object that can interpret dates in the "MM-dd-yyyy" format. The method setLenient(false) is called on this object, ensuring that it will enforce strict adherence to this format.

The date string is then parsed into a Date object, which is set into a GregorianCalendar instance. The GregorianCalendar class includes provisions for leap year handling, which simplifies our job considerably.

The specified number of days is then added to this date, and the result is formatted back into a string and returned.

If the given string cannot be parsed into a date (e.g. it doesn't match the "MM-dd-yyyy" format or the date specified doesn't exist), the parse method will raise an exception, which we catch and return null in response."}]

[{"role": "assistant", "content": "You can solve this problem using a queue to generate a sequence from the input array "A". For each element in "A", add the corresponding element to the queue A[i] times.

Here is a java solution:

```java
import java.util.*;

class RLEIterator {
    Queue<Integer> queue;

    public RLEIterator(int[] A) {
        queue = new LinkedList<>();
        for(int i = 0; i < A.length; i+=2) {
            for(int j = 0; j < A[i]; j++) {
                queue.add(A[i+1]);
            }
        }
    }

    public int next(int n) {
        while(!queue.isEmpty() && n > 0) {
            queue.remove();
            n--;
        }
        return n==0 && !queue.isEmpty() ? queue.peek() : -1;
    }
}
```

However, please note that the above solution can exceed the space and time limits on the given constraints. A more optimal solution would be to store the frequency and value pairs in the array as they are and iterate through them as needed by `next(int n)`. 

Here's a better Java solution:

```java
class RLEIterator {

    int[] A;
    int i, q;

    public RLEIterator(int[] A) {
        this.A = A;
        i = 0;
        q = 0;
    }

    public int next(int n) {
        while (i < A.length) {
            // if the remainder of the current sequence A[i] is greater than n
            if (q + n > A[i]) {
                n -= A[i] - q;
                // move on to the next sequence
                q = 0;
                i += 2;
            } else {
                q += n;
                return A[i+1];
            }
        }
        return -1;
    }
}
```
This optimal solution maintains an index `i` to A, and let `q` be the quantity of that element that is left. When `next(n)` is called, we'll decrement `q` by `n` if possible and return `A[i+1]`.  Otherwise, we'll increment `i` by `2` and make `q = 0` denoting we've used up sequences `i, i+1`."}]

