[{"role": "assistant", "content": "Sure, I can guide you through a thread-safe implementation of the Singleton Design Pattern in Java using Bill Pugh's Singleton approach.

Here's a step by-step guide:

1. Declare a private static class (the "helper class") inside your Singleton class.
2. Have that helper class instantiate a single instance of the Singleton class as a final variable.
3. Return this single instance through a public method on the Singleton class, e.g., `getInstance()`.

Here's an example:

```java
public class Singleton {

    // Step 1: Declare a private static class
    private static class SingletonHelper {
        // Step 2: SingletonHelper class creates a Singleton instance
        private static final Singleton INSTANCE = new Singleton();
    }

    // Declare constructor as private. This prohibits other classes from 
    // instantiating a new object of this class
    private Singleton(){
        // Optimization: Add a protective check to avoid instantiation using reflection
        if (SingletonHelper.INSTANCE != null) {
            throw new RuntimeException("Use getInstance() method to get the single instance of this class.");
        } 
    }

    // Step 3: Provide a public method to get the instance that the SingletonHelper holds.
    // Other classes will use this method to get the Singleton object.
    public static Singleton getInstance() {
        return SingletonHelper.INSTANCE;
    }
}
```
This is known as the "Bill Pugh Singleton" approach and doesn't require synchronization, making it is highly efficient and also thread-safe. The Singleton instance will not be created until needed, and is created by the SingletonHelper class.

For this "Singleton" class, you can only get its instance through the `getInstance()` method. A new instance can't be created directly due to the private constructor, and even Reflection is handled in this constructor.

The instance won't be created until the JVM loads the SingletonHelper class, which will only occur when the `getInstance()` method is called. This is why it's thread-safe (no need for synchronized or volatile)."}]

