        MyClass obj1 = new MyClass();  // Create an object of class MyClass
        System.out.println(obj1.x);  // Output: 5
    }
}
```

2. Parametrized constructor: A constructor which has specific number of parameters.

```Java
public class MyClass {
    int x;

    // constructor with a parameter
    public MyClass(int y) {
        x = y;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        MyClass obj2 = new MyClass(10);
        System.out.println(obj2.x);  // Output: 10
    }
}
``` 

A constructor is called "special method" in Java, as it is used to initialize the state of an object, and it is invoked when the object of the class is created.

Constructors indeed foster efficient instantiation of class entities because they really streamline the process for you. You don't need to have a separate method to initialize your object properties, you simply define a constructor and set the properties in there.

In terms of the implications for object-oriented programming, a constructor is essential for creating an instance of a class. This is crucial in OOP languages like Java where the whole program structure revolves around creating classes and objects for those classes to perform tasks. In Java, every class you create or use is an instance of the Object class, which means a constructor was used at some point to initialize it."}]

