This program uses Java 8's streams to demonstrate the concept. Sequential streams run on a single thread, whereas parallel streams leverage multicore architecture of CPU to perform operations in parallel. Here, we use a filter operation to retrieve numbers divisible by the given input.

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.concurrent.TimeUnit;

public class Main {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        final int size = 1000000;
        final int divisor = 5;

        List<Integer> numbers = new ArrayList<>();
        for (int i = 1; i <= size; i++) {
            numbers.add(i);
        }

        long startSequential = System.nanoTime();
        List<Integer> seqList = numbers.stream()
                .filter(num -> num % divisor == 0)
                .collect(Collectors.toList());
        long endSequential = System.nanoTime();

        long startParallel = System.nanoTime();
        List<Integer> parList = numbers.parallelStream()
                .filter(num -> num % divisor == 0)
                .collect(Collectors.toList());
        long endParallel = System.nanoTime();

        long sequentialTime = TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS.convert((endSequential - startSequential), TimeUnit.NANOSECONDS);
        long parallelTime = TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS.convert((endParallel - startParallel), TimeUnit.NANOSECONDS);

        System.out.println("Sequential Time : " + sequentialTime);
        System.out.println("Parallel Time   : " + parallelTime);
    }

}
```

