  }

  //to pop elements from stack
  public void pop() {
    if(top < 0) {
      System.out.println("Stack Underflow. Cannot pop an element.");
    } else {
      int popped = stackArray[top--];
      System.out.println(popped + " popped from stack.");
    }
  }

  //to display top element of stack
  public void peek() {
    if(top < 0) {
      System.out.println("Stack is empty.");
    } else {
      System.out.println("Top element is " + stackArray[top]);
    }
  }
}
```

You can initialize and use the Stack class in a main function like this:

```java
public class Main {
  public static void main(String[] args) {
    Stack stack = new Stack(5); // creating a stack of size 5

    stack.pop();    // trying to pop an element from empty Stack. It should cause stack underflow.
    stack.push(5);  // should successfully push 5 into stack
    stack.push(6);  // should successfully push 6 into stack
    stack.peek();   // should display "Top element is 6"
    stack.push(7);  // should successfully push 7 into stack
    stack.push(8);  // should successfully push 8 into stack
    stack.push(9);  // should successfully push 9 into stack
    stack.push(10); // trying to push 10 into full Stack. It should cause stack overflow.
  }
}
```

The push operation checks if the stack is full (overflow condition) before trying to insert new elements. Correspondingly, the pop operation verifies if the stack is empty (underflow condition) before attempting to remove an element. The `peek` operation is used to fetch the topmost element of the stack. The error handling mechanisms for stack overflow and underflow conditions are embedded into these operations themselves."}]

