[{"role": "assistant", "content": "Here's a simple implementation of a thread-safe singleton class in Java using double-checked locking:

```java
public class Singleton {
    // Private constructor prevents instantiation from other classes
    private Singleton() {}

    /**
     * SingletonHolder is loaded on the first execution of Singleton.getInstance() 
     * or the first access to SingletonHolder.INSTANCE, not before.
     */
    private static class SingletonHolder {
        private static final Singleton INSTANCE = new Singleton();
    }

    public static Singleton getInstance() {
        return SingletonHolder.INSTANCE;
    }

    private int sharedResource;

    public synchronized void updateSharedResource(int value) {
        sharedResource = value;
    }

    public synchronized int getSharedResource() {
        return sharedResource;
    }
}
```
This approach takes advantage of what’s called "on-demand holder idiom." It doesn’t introduce any additional overhead like synchronized keyword and it is thread-safe.

The key to the double checked locking mechanism is the volatile keyword. Without volatile, there can occur out-of-order write, which can result in seeing partially constructed instance of Singleton.

Now, If you still want to implement double-check locking, your code will be as follows:

```java
public class Singleton {

    private volatile static Singleton singletonInstance;
    private int sharedResource;

    private Singleton(){
    }

    public static Singleton getInstance(){
        if(singletonInstance == null){
            synchronized (Singleton.class){
                if(singletonInstance == null){
                    singletonInstance = new Singleton();
                }
            }
        }
        return singletonInstance;
    }

    public synchronized void updateSharedResource(int value){
        sharedResource = value;
    }

    public synchronized int getSharedResource(){
        return sharedResource;
    }
}
```
In this approach, we first check if an instance is created, if not, then we synchronize. This is called double-checked locking because second check of Singleton instance with lock is done to ensure that Singleton instance is created one and only one time."}]

