[{"role": "assistant", "content": "To solve this problem in Java, you can create a Node class for the singly linked list, then create a method for duplicating the linked list. Here is a solution:

```java
class Node {
    int data;
    Node next;

    Node(int data) {
        this.data = data;
        this.next = null;
    }
}

class LinkedList {
    Node head;

    void duplicate() {
        Node current = this.head;
        Node tail = this.head;

        // Get the tail node and the length of the list.
        while(tail.next != null) {
            tail = tail.next;
        }

        while(current != null) {
            // Create a new node for each node in the list and attach it to the end.
            tail.next = new Node(current.data);
            tail = tail.next;
            current = current.next;
        }
    }

    // Method to print the LinkedList.
    void printList() {
        Node temp = head;
        while (temp != null) {
            System.out.print(temp.data+" ");
            temp = temp.next;
        }
        System.out.println();
    }
}

public class Main{
    public static void main(String[] args){
        LinkedList linkedList = new LinkedList();

        linkedList.head = new Node(1);
        linkedList.head.next = new Node(2);
        linkedList.head.next.next = new Node(3);

        System.out.println("Original LinkedList:");
        linkedList.printList();

        linkedList.duplicate();

        System.out.println("\nDuplicated LinkedList:");
        linkedList.printList();
    }
}
```
