[{"role": "assistant", "content": "```java
import java.util.Set;

public class BipartiteGraph<L, R> {
    private MultiValueMap<L, R> leftMap;
    private MultiValueMap<R, L> rightMap;

    public BipartiteGraph() {
        this.leftMap = new MultiValueMap<>();
        this.rightMap = new MultiValueMap<>();
    }

    /**
     * Replaces the left side of the graph with the values inside the provided set.
     * @param values the set of values to replace the left side of the graph
     */
    public void replaceLeftSide(Set<L> values) {
        // Clear the existing leftMap
        leftMap.clear();

        // Add the new values to the leftMap
        for (L value : values) {
            leftMap.put(value, new HashSet<>());  // Initialize an empty set for each value
        }

        // Update the rightMap to remove any references to the old left side values
        for (R rightValue : rightMap.keySet()) {
            Set<L> connectedLeftValues = rightMap.get(rightValue);
            connectedLeftValues.retainAll(values);  // Retain only the values that are still present in the new left side
        }
    }
}
```

In the solution, the `replaceLeftSide` method clears the existing leftMap and then adds the new values to it. It also updates the rightMap to remove any references to the old left side values that are not present in the new set of values. This ensures that the bipartite graph is updated correctly when the left side is replaced with a new set of values."}]

